      "nets": ["MISO", "MOSI", "SDA", "PWM"],
      "observed": "J4 (AVR-ISP-6) pins 1 and 4 are labeled MISO and MOSI, but U2 pin 5 (PB0) is labeled SDA, U2 pin 6 (PB1) is labeled PWM, U3 pin 5 (PB0) is labeled SDA, and U3 pin 6 (PB1) is labeled PWM. No MISO or MOSI labels appear anywhere else on the sheet.",
      "expected": "The ISP header's MISO and MOSI pins must connect to the MCU's MISO (PB1) and MOSI (PB0) pins for in-system programming.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "schematic",
          "quote": "J4 AVR-ISP-6 pins: 1 MISO, 4 MOSI; U2 pins: 5 SDA, 6 PWM; U3 pins: 5 SDA, 6 PWM"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The board cannot be programmed via the ISP header because MISO and MOSI are floating; this makes the MCU unprogrammable unless an alternative programming method is used.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "category": "wrong_rating",
      "components": ["R6"],
      "pins": ["R6.1", "R6.2", "U2.3", "U3.3"],
      "nets": ["TACH", "VCC", "+5V"],
      "observed": "R6 (10K) is connected between the TACH net and the VCC rail. The TACH net connects to U2 pin 3 (PB4), U3 pin 3 (PB4), and J2 pin 3 (TACH_IN).",
      "expected": "The TACH pull-up should be connected to the +5V logic supply, not the raw VCC input, to avoid exposing the MCU pin to a voltage above its supply.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "schematic",
          "quote": "R6 10K with VCC symbol on one side and TACH label on the other; TACH connects to U2 pin 3 and U3 pin 3"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "If VCC is higher than +5V (which it must be for the LM1117-5.0 to regulate), the TACH input will see VCC voltage, likely exceeding the ATtiny's absolute maximum rating and damaging the MCU.",
      "confidence": 0.9
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["Q1", "Q2", "R2"],
      "pins": ["Q2.1", "Q1.1", "R2.1", "R2.2"],
      "nets": ["PWM", "VCC_OUT", "+5V"],
      "observed": "R2 (1K) connects the PWM net to the base of Q2. Q2's collector drives the gate of P-MOSFET Q1, which switches the fan power (VCC_OUT) to J2 pin 2. Thus the fan power is switched at the PWM frequency.",
      "expected": "For a 4-wire fan, the power (VCC_OUT) should be continuously supplied, and only the PWM input (J2 pin 4) should be modulated. Q1/Q2 should either be omitted or controlled by a separate enable signal, not by the PWM signal.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "schematic",
          "quote": "R2 1K from PWM to Q2 base; Q2 collector to Q1 gate; Q1 drain to VCC_OUT (J2 pin 2); R5 1K from PWM to J2 pin 4 (PWM_OUT)"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The fan's power is interrupted at the PWM frequency, so the fan cannot operate correctly; it will likely run only when PWM is high and may not respond to duty-cycle speed control.",
      "confidence": 0.75
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic contains multiple design errors: the ISP header's MISO/MOSI are not connected to either MCU, the TACH pull-up is tied to the raw VCC rail instead of +5V, and the fan power switch is driven by the PWM signal, which would prevent proper 4-wire fan control."
}

